// ## 2. install.sh verification paths (read, then traced)

// fetch_and_verify (install.sh:399-427) runs in a background subshell
// and records status files (dl/sha/vrc/vhash/size); it always
// `return 0` — verdict lives in the files, not the exit code.
// install_component (:468-540) branches on them:

// | Condition | Behavior | Verdict |
// |-----------|----------|---------|
// | vrc==0 && -x dest | install, "SHA-256 verified" | PASS |
// | dl != 200, existing dest | preserves prior binary, warns | acceptable |
// | dl == 404 | "not yet shipped" skip | acceptable |
// | dl != 200, no binary, core key | exit 1 — refuses partial install | fail-closed |
// | sha missing / != 200 | "missing SHA-256 sidecar; refuse unsigned install", return 1 | fail-closed |
// | expected != actual | "checksum mismatch", return 1 | fail-closed |
// | no sha256sum/shasum | pre-flight need_fail=1 (:257-258) | fail-closed |

// Residual: sidecar travels the same HTTPS release channel as the
// binary (hash-of-the-same-origin, not an independent trust root).
// catalog/ already has index.minisig + registry.pub — a minisign
// sidecar would harden this; NOTE, not a violation of current law.

// ## 3. Host binary-parity measurement — two runs (installer re-ran)

//   Run A (~05:40):
//     dist/ooda            sha 9a922dd5…
//     ~/.openooda/bin/ooda sha 41f2d583… (release download + sidecar)
//     ~/.local/bin/ooda    sha 23e5c3f0… 596,856 B, 2026-09-09
//
//   Run B (~05:50, after user re-ran the installer):
//     dist/ooda            sha bce47457… == ~/.openooda/bin/ooda
//                          — dist-vs-installed parity now HOLDS.
//     ~/.local/bin/ooda    sha 23e5c3f0… UNCHANGED — still shadowing.
//     ooda.tmp.sha256      still records 41f2d583… — the installed
//                          file is now a local build, so the sidecar
//                          no longer describes it (provenance drift).

// PATH: ~/.local/bin (pos 1) precedes ~/.openooda/bin (pos 3);
// `which -a ooda` still resolves ~/.local/bin/ooda FIRST. Same stale
// set for oodac, ooda-lsp, ooda-mcp, blackbox, opm + oodac.bak (all
// 2026-09-09). Re-install refreshed ~/.openooda/bin but never touched
// the shadow set — the migration gap is confirmed reproducible:
// install.sh:551-553 repairs OODA_COMPILER rc references; nothing
// removes the old binaries.

// Live-mutation note: ~/.openooda/bin/ooda was rewritten twice during
// the audit window (04:59→05:37→05:44) — concurrent install/build
// activity while auditing (§1.6 coordination).

// ## 4. Findings by severity

// CRITICAL (1):
//   C1. Binary Parity Law violated on-host (RULES §1.10 as amended,
//       §1.21): post-reinstall, dist==installed holds for ooda, but
//       the stale 2026-09-09 ~/.local/bin toolchain (6 tools + bak)
//       still shadows the certified set on PATH. The law's intent —
//       "the bit-identical binary installed for the user" is the one
//       executed — fails while the shadow exists. Supply-chain
//       property: the certified build is not the one PATH serves.

// MINOR (2):
//   m1. install.sh has no stale-shadow migration: it repairs
//       OODA_COMPILER rc lines (:551-553) but never removes the old
//       ~/.local/bin/ooda* binaries it once installed there.
//   m2. Provenance drift: after a local-build overwrite of
//       ~/.openooda/bin/ooda (05:44), ooda.tmp.sha256 still records
//       the release hash — the record and the file diverge silently.

// RESOLVED (post-review fix, this session):
//   m3 → FIXED. harness_wire.oo deleted (dead code from the removed
//       harness-wiring feature, commit 020693a; latent defects noted:
//       whole-file JSON "overwrite" merge + vacuous ProcessCap stubs).
//       ANCHOR.oo and README.md synced to toolchain-only reality.
//       Remainder for mcode (active install.sh lane): the stale
//       "python3 only needed for harness wiring" comment at
//       install.sh:254 — python3 is actually still used at :186 for
//       release-tag JSON parse.

// ## 5. Recommended repairs (install lane)

// 1. Add a shadow-detection step to install.sh: enumerate every
//    `which -a <tool>` and warn/fail when a non-$BIN_DIR copy
//    outranks the installed one (C1/m1).
// 2. Decide the single bin dir; either symlink the other location
//    or delete stale copies with user consent (C1).
// 3. Consider a minisign sidecar channel (registry.pub exists in
//    catalog/) so verification has a root independent of the
//    release origin (residual hardening).
